# Structural Mechanics & Dynamics — Experimental Analysis

**Grade Awarded:** 1st Class  
**Course:** Structural Mechanics and Dynamics 3 (MECE09036)  
**Institution:** University of Edinburgh  

---

## Executive Summary & Core Experiments

This laboratory workbook evaluates structural deflection mechanics across asymmetric and curved beam geometries, comparing theoretical elastic formulations against experimental measurements[cite: 9].

### Experiment 1: Unsymmetric Bending of Beams
* **Objective:** Investigated the combined vertical ($\delta_v$) and horizontal ($\delta_h$) tip deflections of a mild-steel L-section cantilever beam rotated in $10^\circ$ increments through $180^\circ$ under a constant $5.98\text{ N}$ load[cite: 9].
* **Key Results:** Identified the experimental principal axis angle at $\alpha_{\text{exp}} = 47.1^\circ$, showing strong agreement with the theoretical prediction of $\alpha_{\text{theo}} = 44.6^\circ$ ($5.4\%$ error)[cite: 9].

### Experiment 2: Load-Deflection Behavior of Curved Beams
* **Objective:** Evaluated non-linear stress distribution and elastic stiffness of a semicircular curved beam ($R = 148.65\text{ mm}$) subjected to incremental loading ($0\text{ to }10\text{ N}$) and unloading[cite: 9].
* **Key Results:** Used Castigliano's second theorem to calculate theoretical stiffness values ($k_v = 3.26\text{ N/mm}$, $k_h = 2.56\text{ N/mm}$) and analyzed experimental deviations caused by clamp compliance and support flexibility[cite: 9].

